        </div>
      </div>
    </section><section id="destinations" class="section-alt"><div class="container"><div class="max-w-3xl mb-10 reveal"><span class="section-eyebrow">Explore</span><h2 class="heading-lg text-3xl md:text-4xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Where Should You Go?</h2><p class="body-text text-lg">Explore each city in depth: major attractions with prices and insider tips, day-by-day itineraries, where to stay, what to eat, best photo spots, shopping, nightlife, and essential safety advice.</p></div><div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 md:gap-5 mb-10">
{destinations.map((d, i) => (
<a href="#dest-tabs" data-tab={i} class="dest-card group relative block overflow-hidden rounded-2xl aspect-square no-underline">
<img src={d.image} alt={d.name + " travel guide"} class="h-full w-full object-cover transition-transform duration-500 ease-out group-hover:scale-105" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
<div class="absolute inset-0 bg-gradient-to-t from-black/85 via-black/30 to-transparent"></div>
<div class="absolute bottom-0 left-0 right-0 p-4 md:p-5">
<div class="text-[10px] md:text-[11px] uppercase tracking-[0.15em] text-white/75 font-semibold">{d.bestFor}</div>
<h3 class="font-display text-xl md:text-2xl text-white font-semibold leading-tight mt-1">{d.name}</h3>
<div class="text-xs text-white/70 mt-1.5">{d.dayCount} &middot; {d.bestTime}</div>
</div>
<div class="absolute top-3 right-3 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-white/15 backdrop-blur-sm flex items-center justify-center opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 transition-opacity duration-300">
<svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="white" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5"/></svg>
</div>
</a>
))}
</div>
<div class="dest-tabs" id="dest-tabs"><div class="dest-tab-nav mb-8 overflow-x-auto"><button data-tab="0" class="dest-tab-btn">Beijing</button><button data-tab="1" class="dest-tab-btn">Shanghai</button><button data-tab="2" class="dest-tab-btn">Xi&#39;an</button><button data-tab="3" class="dest-tab-btn">Chengdu</button><button data-tab="4" class="dest-tab-btn">Zhangjiajie</button><button data-tab="5" class="dest-tab-btn">Guilin &amp; Yangshuo</button><button data-tab="6" class="dest-tab-btn">Yunnan</button><button data-tab="7" class="dest-tab-btn">Guangzhou</button></div><div data-tab="0" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Beijing <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">北京</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Beijing is China&#39;s imperial capital and the political and cultural heart of the nation. With over 3,000 years of history, the city layers ancient dynasties alongside cutting-edge modernity. Seven UNESCO World Heritage sites sit within city limits — more than any other city on earth. The Forbidden City alone spans 180 acres with 980 buildings, housing the world&#39;s largest collection of preserved ancient wooden structures. Beyond the monuments, Beijing&#39;s soul lives in its hutongs — narrow alleyways where families have lived for generations, where you can still find old men playing chess over jasmine tea and the scent of jianbing (savory crepes) drifting from street carts. Beijing is also China&#39;s best food city, not for innovation but for authenticity — this is where Peking Duck was perfected, where imperial cuisine meets street food, and where every alley hides a decades-old family recipe.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">History + Great Wall</span><span class="tag">3–5 days</span><span class="tag">Apr–May, Sep–Oct</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">History buffs</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">First-time China visitors</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Families</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Food lovers</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Peking Youth Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Dongcheng District &middot; $15–30/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">The Orchid Hotel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Gulou / Nanluoguxiang &middot; $80–150/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Aman at Summer Palace</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Haidian District &middot; $500+/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Peking Duck</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Siji Minfu (四季民福)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Dengshikou branch has Forbidden City corner tower view</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhajiangmian</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Old Beijing Noodle King (老北京炸酱面大王)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Hearty noodle dish, ¥20–30</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Mongolian Hot Pot</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Donglaishun (东来顺)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Historic brand since 1903, mutton specialty</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1.5h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Mutianyu Great Wall</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Restored, cable car, toboggan slide</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Gubei Water Town</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canal town with Simatai Great Wall backdrop</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2.5h by train</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Chengde Mountain Resort</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Qing dynasty imperial summer palace</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Forbidden City <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">故宫</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The world&#39;s largest palace complex, home to 24 emperors across the Ming and Qing dynasties. Built between 1406-1420, it covers 72 hectares with 980 surviving buildings. The scale is staggering — the central axis alone stretches nearly a kilometer from the Meridian Gate to the Imperial Garden. Key halls: Hall of Supreme Harmony (the throne room with its golden dragon ceiling), Palace of Heavenly Purity (emperor&#39;s living quarters), and the Treasure Gallery housing imperial jewelry and artifacts. Pro tip: the western and eastern palaces are quieter and more atmospheric than the central axis.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥60 (Apr–Oct), ¥40 (Nov–Mar). Treasure Gallery: ¥10 extra. Clock Gallery: ¥10 extra</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:30–17:00 (Apr–Oct), 8:30–16:30 (Nov–Mar). Closed Mondays</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Book online 1-7 days in advance — tickets sell out. Enter through Meridian Gate (south), exit through Gate of Divine Prowess (north) directly into Jingshan Park. Allow 3-4 hours minimum. Audio guide ¥40 (well worth it). Avoid Chinese holidays when crowd levels exceed 80,000 visitors per day.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Great Wall at Mutianyu <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">慕田峪长城</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The most visitor-friendly Great Wall section near Beijing. Fully restored with smooth stone paths, 23 watchtowers, and stunning mountain scenery. Less crowded than Badaling (the tour-bus section) but equally impressive. The 2.5km restored section winds dramatically along mountain ridges, with dense forest on both sides turning brilliant colors in autumn. The cable car whisks you to Tower 14, from where you can hike west to Tower 23 (the highest point) or east to Tower 6 (where the toboggan slide starts).</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥45 entrance. Cable car: ¥120 round-trip, ¥100 single. Toboggan: ¥100</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:00–17:00 (Mar–Nov), 8:30–16:30 (Dec–Feb)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go early (arrive by 8am) to have the wall nearly to yourself. The hike from Tower 14 to Tower 23 is about 1 hour each way with steep sections — wear good shoes. The toboggan slide down is genuinely fun and worth the queue. In winter, the wall dusted with snow is magical but icy — bring traction devices.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Temple of Heaven <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">天坛</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A masterpiece of Ming dynasty architecture where emperors performed annual ceremonies to pray for good harvests. The Hall of Prayer for Good Harvests — a triple-tiered circular wooden structure built entirely without nails — is one of the most photographed buildings in China. The surrounding 267-hectare park is a living museum of Beijing life: at dawn, locals gather to practice tai chi, sword dancing, water calligraphy on the stone paths, and group singing. It&#39;s as much a cultural experience as an architectural one.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥35 (Apr–Oct), ¥30 (Nov–Mar). Through-ticket including all halls: ¥40</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">6:00–21:00 (park), 8:00–17:30 (halls)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go at sunrise (6-8am) to see locals doing morning exercises — this is the real Beijing. The Echo Wall and Circular Mound Altar have fascinating acoustic properties. Allow 2-3 hours including park time. Combine with a visit to the nearby Hongqiao Pearl Market for souvenir shopping.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Summer Palace <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">颐和园</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The largest and best-preserved imperial garden in China, and a UNESCO World Heritage site. Built as a royal summer retreat, it centers on Kunming Lake (man-made, dug by 10,000 laborers) and Longevity Hill, crowned by the Tower of Buddhist Incense with panoramic views. The Long Corridor — a 728-meter covered walkway decorated with over 14,000 paintings of Chinese mythology and landscapes — holds a Guinness World Record. Rent a paddle boat on the lake or hike the hill paths for quieter corners.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥30 (Apr–Oct), ¥20 (Nov–Mar). Combined ticket: ¥60</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">6:30–18:00 (Apr–Oct), 7:00–17:00 (Nov–Mar)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Allow 3-4 hours. The walk around the entire lake takes about 2 hours and offers the best photo angles of the Tower of Buddhist Incense. Avoid summer weekends when Beijing families flock here. The Marble Boat at the northwest corner is a quirky Qing dynasty folly — a stone boat pavilion that Empress Dowager Cixi had built using funds meant for the navy.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Nanluoguxiang &amp; Hutongs <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">南锣鼓巷</span></h5></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Beijing&#39;s most famous hutong (alleyway) neighborhood, dating to the Yuan dynasty (13th century). The main 800-meter lane is lined with courtyard cafés, craft shops, and snack stalls — touristy but lively. The magic is in the side alleys (east and west off the main lane) where real Beijingers still live in traditional courtyard homes (siheyuan). You&#39;ll see residents playing mahjong, tending rooftop gardens, and walking tiny dogs. Nearby, the Drum and Bell Towers mark the historic center of the old city, and Houhai Lake offers lakeside bars and boat rides.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free to wander. Drum Tower: ¥20. Bell Tower: ¥15</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Always open. Shops generally 10:00–22:00</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The main lane gets packed on weekends — go on a weekday morning or duck into side alleys immediately. The area around Beiluoguxiang (north of the main lane) is less touristy with excellent craft coffee shops. Rent a bicycle to explore the wider hutong network — the grid pattern makes it easy to navigate.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>3 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Imperial Beijing</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tiananmen Square → Forbidden City (3h, ¥60) → Jingshan Park sunset → Peking Duck dinner at Siji Minfu</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Great Wall</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Mutianyu Great Wall full day (1.5h drive, ¥45) — cable car up, toboggan down → Guijie food street</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Temples &amp; Hutongs</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Temple of Heaven (2h, ¥35) → Nanluoguxiang Hutong walk → Houhai Lake → Departure</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>5 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Imperial Beijing</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tiananmen Square → Forbidden City → Jingshan Park → Wangfujing night market</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Great Wall</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Mutianyu Great Wall full day → Evening Peking Duck at Dadong (大董)</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Temples &amp; Parks</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Temple of Heaven → Summer Palace (3h, ¥30) — boat ride on Kunming Lake</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Art &amp; Culture</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">798 Art District → Lama Temple (Yonghegong, ¥25) → Guijie food street</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 5</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Day Trip</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Gubei Water Town (2h) — Simatai Great Wall at sunset, or Ming Tombs + Sacred Way</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Jingshan Park summit at sunset — panoramic view of the Forbidden City with the CBD skyline behind it</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Mutianyu Great Wall at Tower 20 looking west along the ridge — best in autumn when the forest turns gold and red</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Temple of Heaven at sunrise — catch locals doing tai chi with the Hall of Prayer silhouetted against the morning sky</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Beijing&#39;s shopping ranges from sprawling wholesale markets to cutting-edge designer boutiques. For traditional crafts: Panjiayuan Antique Market (weekends only) for calligraphy, jade, Cultural Revolution memorabilia, and genuine antiques — arrive early (by 7am) for the best finds and be prepared to bargain hard. For tea: Maliandao Tea Street has hundreds of tea shops where you can sample before buying. For modern fashion and design: Taikoo Li Sanlitun for international brands and Beijing&#39;s hippest architecture. For quirky souvenirs: 798 Art District has artist-designed goods you won&#39;t find elsewhere. Hongqiao Pearl Market (near Temple of Heaven) is tourist-oriented but convenient for pearls, silk, and electronics. Bargaining rule across all markets: start at 20-30% of the asking price and settle around 40-50%.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Beijing&#39;s nightlife splits into several zones. Sanlitun is the expat and clubbing hub — Migas rooftop bar has the best skyline views, while Dada is the underground electronic music institution. Gulou (Drum Tower area) has a more bohemian vibe: Great Leap Brewing for craft beer in a converted factory, Modernista for live jazz and swing dancing, and countless tiny hutong bars with rooftop terraces in summer. For a uniquely Beijing experience: Laoshe Teahouse for traditional performances (acrobatics, opera, face-changing) with tea and snacks in a historic setting. Ghost Street (Guijie) stays open until 4am with hundreds of restaurants lit by red lanterns — the place for late-night spicy crayfish and cold beer.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$35–50/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$80–120/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$250+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Chinese New Year (Spring Festival)</span><span class="tag">Late Jan/Feb</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">The city empties as millions return home, but temple fairs at Ditan Park and Longtan Park offer folk performances, snacks, and festive chaos. Expect fireworks (legal within 5th Ring Road on New Year&#39;s Eve only). Many restaurants close for 3-7 days.</p></div><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">National Day Golden Week</span><span class="tag">Oct 1–7</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">China&#39;s busiest travel period. Tiananmen Square is decorated with massive floral displays. All attractions are extremely crowded — book everything weeks in advance or avoid Beijing entirely during this week.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Capital Airport (PEK): Airport Express ¥25 (20 min). Daxing (PKX): Express ¥35 (30 min). Taxi ~¥120 to city. Metro ¥3–9, covers all sites.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Beijing is extremely safe for tourists — violent crime is virtually nonexistent. The main risks are petty: pickpocketing in crowded tourist areas (Forbidden City, Wangfujing) and taxi scams at the airport (always use the official taxi queue or Didi). Watch for the &#39;tea house scam&#39; where friendly English-speaking strangers invite you to a traditional tea ceremony that ends with an extortionate bill. Air quality can be poor November–March: check the AQI and bring N95 masks if needed. Tap water is not drinkable — use bottled water for everything including brushing teeth.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="1" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Shanghai <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">上海</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Shanghai is China&#39;s future and its past colliding in real time. On one side of the Huangpu River, the Bund&#39;s 1920s Art Deco landmarks tell the story of old Shanghai — the &#39;Paris of the East&#39; where European powers carved out concessions and built one of the world&#39;s great trading ports. On the other side, Pudong&#39;s sci-fi skyline — anchored by the Shanghai Tower, the world&#39;s third-tallest building at 632 meters — represents China&#39;s ambition in glass and steel. Between these poles, Shanghai offers tree-lined French Concession streets with Europe&#39;s best-preserved Art Deco residential architecture outside of Paris, a food scene that ranges from ¥15 soup dumplings to three-Michelin-star tasting menus, and an energy that makes New York feel sleepy. Shanghai is not ancient China — it&#39;s China right now, and it&#39;s exhilarating.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">City + Disney</span><span class="tag">2–4 days</span><span class="tag">Mar–May, Oct–Nov</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">City lovers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Foodies</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Architecture enthusiasts</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">First-time China visitors</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Nightlife</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Mingtown Hiker Youth Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Near People&#39;s Square &middot; $12–25/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">URBN Boutique Hotel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Jing&#39;an District &middot; $80–150/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">The Peninsula Shanghai</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">The Bund &middot; $400+/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Xiaolongbao (Soup Dumplings)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Jia Jia Tang Bao (佳家汤包)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Legendary soup dumplings, expect a queue</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Shengjianbao (Pan-fried Buns)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Da Hu Chun (大壶春)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Crispy-bottom pork buns, Shanghai classic</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hairy Crab (seasonal)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Cheng Long Hang (成隆行)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Autumn only (Sep–Nov), a Shanghainese obsession</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhujiajiao Water Town</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Ancient canal town with stone bridges and boat rides</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">25 min by high-speed rail</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Suzhou</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Classical gardens (UNESCO), silk museums</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">45 min by high-speed rail</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Hangzhou</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">West Lake, tea plantations, Lingyin Temple</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">The Bund &amp; Huangpu River <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">外滩</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A 1.5km waterfront promenade along the Huangpu River, lined with 26 buildings representing every major architectural style from Gothic to Art Deco — the legacy of Shanghai&#39;s colonial trading era. By day, it&#39;s a riverfront walkway where locals fly kites and practice tai chi. By night, it transforms into one of the world&#39;s most spectacular cityscapes as Pudong&#39;s skyscrapers light up across the river. The view encompasses the Oriental Pearl Tower, Shanghai Tower, Shanghai World Financial Center (the &#39;bottle opener&#39;), and Jin Mao Tower — four of the world&#39;s tallest buildings in a single frame.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free. Huangpu River cruise: ¥120–180</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Always open. Best for photos: 30 minutes before sunset through blue hour</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go at 5am for sunrise tai chi with locals, or at 7pm for the light show. The ferry crossing to Pudong costs ¥2 and gives the best river-level views of both skylines — take it from the Jinling East Road ferry pier.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Shanghai Tower <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">上海中心大厦</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The world&#39;s third-tallest building (632m, 128 floors) and China&#39;s tallest. The observation deck on the 118th-119th floors offers 360-degree views from 546 meters — on clear days, you can see the Yangtze River delta. The elevator is the world&#39;s fastest at 20.5 m/s, reaching the top in 55 seconds. The building itself is a twisting glass spiral designed to reduce wind loads by 24% — a feat of engineering as impressive as the view.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥180 (adult), ¥120 (student/senior)</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:30–22:00 (last entry 21:00)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go on a clear day — Shanghai haze can obscure the view. Sunset tickets sell out, book online in advance. For a cheaper alternative, the bar at the Park Hyatt on floors 87-93 of the neighboring SWFC has similar views with a drink in hand (¥100+ per cocktail).</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yu Garden &amp; Old City <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">豫园</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A 400-year-old classical Chinese garden in the heart of Shanghai&#39;s old city, built by a Ming dynasty official for his parents. Its 2 hectares are a masterclass in Chinese garden design: rockeries meant to evoke mountain ranges, carp-filled ponds, zigzag bridges (to confuse evil spirits, which can only travel in straight lines), and the iconic Mid-Lake Pavilion teahouse. The surrounding Yuyuan Bazaar is a restored Ming-Qing style shopping area — touristy but photogenic, especially during lantern festival season.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥40 (Apr–Nov), ¥30 (Dec–Mar)</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:45–16:45 (last entry 16:15)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go when it opens to avoid crowds. The nearby City God Temple (Chenghuang Miao) is free and more authentic. Skip the overpriced restaurants in the bazaar and walk 10 minutes to the real local food streets around Yunnan Road or the less-touristy Dongjiadu Road area for authentic Shanghai breakfast foods.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">French Concession <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">法租界</span></h5></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Shanghai&#39;s most charming neighborhood — tree-lined streets, 1920s lane houses, and the best concentration of cafés, boutiques, and restaurants in the city. The area was a French-governed concession from 1849 to 1943, and the European architectural legacy remains beautifully preserved. Key streets: Wukang Road (historic mansions including the Normandie Apartments), Fuxing Road (cycling under plane trees), and Anfu Road (designer boutiques and third-wave coffee). Fuxing Park fills with locals doing morning tai chi, ballroom dancing, and calligraphy.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free to wander</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Always open. Shops and cafés generally 9:00–22:00</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Rent a bicycle — the grid of plane-tree-lined streets is ideal for aimless exploration. The Propaganda Poster Art Centre (¥25) on Huashan Road is a fascinating, hidden-in-a-basement museum of Mao-era propaganda art. Evening: craft cocktails at Speak Low (hidden behind a bookshelf, consistently ranked among Asia&#39;s best bars).</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>2 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Bund &amp; Pudong</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Morning walk along the Bund → cross to Pudong via ferry (¥2) → Shanghai Tower observation deck (¥180) → Nanjing Road stroll</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Old &amp; New</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Yu Garden (¥40) → French Concession tree-lined streets → Tianzifang art lanes → Xintiandi dinner</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>4 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Bund + Pudong</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bund morning → Pudong Lujiazui → Shanghai Tower (world&#39;s 3rd tallest) → Huangpu River night cruise</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Culture Day</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Shanghai Museum (free) → People&#39;s Square → Yu Garden → Old City Bazaar</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Disney / Water Town</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Shanghai Disney Resort full day (¥475+), or Zhujiajiao Water Town (1h, ¥30) with canal boat ride</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Local Life</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">French Concession cafés → Fuxing Park tai chi → Tianzifang → M50 art district → Xintiandi</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The Bund at blue hour (7-7:30pm in summer) — Pudong skyline reflection in the river with lights coming on</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Wukang Road and the Normandie Apartments intersection — 1920s Shanghai at its most cinematic</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Longtang (lane house) alleyways in the Former French Concession — especially on Yongkang Road or Jianguo West Road at golden hour</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Shanghai is China&#39;s shopping capital. For luxury: Nanjing Road West (not East — East is tourist-oriented) has every global flagship brand. For local designers and cool boutiques: the French Concession streets around Julu Road, Fumin Road, and Xinle Road. For tech and gadgets: the multiple floors of the Pacific Digital Plaza or the electronics market at Metro City in Xujiahui (bargain fiercely). For fabrics and custom tailoring: the South Bund Fabric Market — bring a photo of what you want and get suits, dresses, or coats made in 2-3 days for a fraction of Western prices. For traditional crafts and antiques: Dongtai Road Antique Market (mornings only) for Cultural Revolution memorabilia, jade, and old Shanghai advertising posters. Taikang Road/Tianzifang in the French Concession is touristy but photogenic — narrow lanes packed with craft shops, galleries, and bars in converted Shikumen (stone-gate) houses.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Shanghai&#39;s nightlife rivals any global city. The Bund: rooftop bars with million-dollar views — Flair at the Ritz-Carlton and VUE Bar at the Hyatt on the Bund are the classics. French Concession: hidden speakeasies and craft cocktail bars — Speak Low, Sober Company, and Union Trading Co. consistently rank among Asia&#39;s 50 Best Bars. For live music: JZ Club for world-class jazz, Yuyintang for indie and punk, MAO Livehouse for touring acts. For clubbing: Found 158 (a sunken entertainment complex off Julu Road) has a cluster of clubs including 44KW and Elevator for underground electronic music. For something uniquely Shanghai: the Peace Hotel&#39;s Old Jazz Band — a septuagenarian sextet that&#39;s been playing 1920s jazz nightly since 1980 in an Art Deco jewel box of a bar.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$40–60/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$100–160/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$300+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Shanghai International Film Festival</span><span class="tag">June</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">One of Asia&#39;s largest film festivals with screenings across the city. International and Chinese cinema, often with English subtitles.</p></div><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Mid-Autumn Festival</span><span class="tag">Sep/Oct</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">The Old City and Yu Garden are decorated with lanterns. Try mooncakes from local bakeries — the Shanghai-style with pork filling is a local specialty.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Pudong (PVG): Maglev ¥50 (8 min) then metro. Hongqiao (SHA): Metro Line 2 direct (¥5). Metro ¥3–9, 19 lines. Taxi: ¥14 flagfall.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Shanghai is extremely safe. Pickpocketing is the main concern on Nanjing Road East and around Yu Garden. Taxis are reliable and metered but drivers rarely speak English — have your destination written in Chinese. The metro system is modern, clean, and has English signage throughout. Avoid unlicensed taxis at the airport and train stations. Tap water is not drinkable. Summers (July-August) are oppressively hot and humid — carry water and plan indoor activities during midday.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="2" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Xi&#39;an <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">西安</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Xi&#39;an is where Chinese civilization begins. As the capital of 13 dynasties spanning over 1,100 years, it was the eastern terminus of the Silk Road and — at its Tang dynasty peak — the largest and most cosmopolitan city in the world, with over a million residents from across Asia, the Middle East, and beyond. Today, Xi&#39;an is famous for one thing above all: the Terracotta Warriors, an army of 8,000 life-sized clay soldiers buried with China&#39;s first emperor in 210 BCE. But Xi&#39;an is far more than its greatest archaeological discovery. The 14km Ming dynasty city wall — the most complete ancient city wall in China — encircles a vibrant old city where the Muslim Quarter&#39;s food stalls serve lamb skewers and hand-pulled noodles under red lanterns, and where you can bicycle atop fortifications built 600 years ago while looking down at everyday life unfolding below.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Terracotta Warriors</span><span class="tag">2–3 days</span><span class="tag">Mar–May, Sep–Nov</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">History lovers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Food travelers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Photographers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Silk Road enthusiasts</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Shuyuan Youth Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Near South Gate (Nanmen) &middot; $10–20/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Eastern House Boutique Hotel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Inside City Wall &middot; $50–100/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Wyndham Grand Xi&#39;an South</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Near Giant Wild Goose Pagoda &middot; $100–200/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yangrou Paomo (Lamb Soup with Bread)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tongshengxiang (同盛祥)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Xi&#39;an&#39;s signature dish, crumble bread into lamb broth</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Biangbiang Noodles</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Muslim Quarter stalls (biangbiang面)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Thick hand-pulled noodles with chili oil</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Roujiamo (Chinese Burger)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Fan Ji (樊记)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Braised pork in flatbread, Xi&#39;an fast food</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1.5h by train</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Mount Hua (Huashan)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Sacred mountain, thrilling cliffside plank walk</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Famen Temple</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Buddhist temple with Sakyamuni finger-bone relic</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Terracotta Warriors Museum <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">兵马俑</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">One of the world&#39;s greatest archaeological discoveries, found by farmers digging a well in 1974. The army was created around 210 BCE to guard Qin Shi Huang — China&#39;s first emperor — in the afterlife. Pit 1 (the largest) contains over 6,000 warriors and horses in battle formation, each with unique facial features and hairstyles indicating their rank. The scale is overwhelming: row after row of warriors stretching into the distance. Pit 2 contains cavalry and archers, while Pit 3 is the command center. The bronze chariots in the exhibition hall are masterpieces of ancient metallurgy.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥120 (Mar–Nov), ¥100 (Dec–Feb)</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:30–18:00 (Mar–Nov), 8:30–17:30 (Dec–Feb)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go to Pit 1 last, not first — start with Pit 2 and 3, then the exhibition hall, then Pit 1 for the climactic reveal. Arrive early (8:30am) or late afternoon to avoid tour groups. The museum is 30km east of Xi&#39;an — take public bus 306 (Tourist Bus 5) from Xi&#39;an Railway Station east square for ¥7, or a Didi for about ¥150 round-trip.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Ancient City Wall <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">城墙</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The most complete ancient city fortification in China — a 14km rectangular wall averaging 12 meters high and 15 meters wide at the top. Originally built during the Tang dynasty and rebuilt in the Ming dynasty (1370), it encircles the old city center. The top is wide enough for a dedicated bike path — cycling the full circuit takes 1.5-2 hours and offers constantly shifting views of old and new Xi&#39;an: temple roofs on one side, modern high-rises on the other.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥54. Bike rental: ¥45 (3 hours). Electric cart: ¥80 per section</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:00–22:00 (South Gate until midnight in summer)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Rent a bike from the official rental stations on the wall, not from vendors outside. Start at the South Gate (Nanmen) — it&#39;s the most impressive entry point with the largest gate complex. Go in late afternoon to catch sunset from the wall. The section between South Gate and West Gate has the best views of the old city.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Muslim Quarter <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">回民街</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Xi&#39;an&#39;s culinary heart and the legacy of its Silk Road history. This neighborhood behind the Drum Tower has been home to Xi&#39;an&#39;s Hui Muslim community for over 1,300 years, since Arab and Persian traders settled here during the Tang dynasty. The main street is a sensory overload of food stalls, spice shops, and butcher shops displaying hanging lamb carcasses — but the real treasures are on the side alleys where local families run tiny eateries unchanged for generations.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free entry</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Always open. Food stalls operate roughly 10:00–midnight</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Ignore the main street after the first block — dive into the side alleys. Must-try dishes: yangrou paomo (crumbled flatbread in lamb soup), biangbiang noodles (belt-wide noodles with chili oil), roujiamo (Chinese &#39;burger&#39;), and persimmon cakes. The Great Mosque (¥25) hidden in the quarter is one of China&#39;s oldest and largest mosques, blending Chinese temple architecture with Islamic function — a unique architectural hybrid.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>2 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Terracotta Warriors</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Terracotta Army Museum (3h, ¥120) — pits 1, 2, 3 → Muslim Quarter evening food crawl</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">City Wall &amp; Pagodas</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bike the Ancient City Wall (14km, ¥54, 2h) → Giant Wild Goose Pagoda → Great Tang All Day Mall at night</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>3 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Terracotta Warriors</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Terracotta Army (full morning) → Huaqing Palace hot springs → Muslim Quarter dinner</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">City Wall</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bike the City Wall → Shaanxi History Museum → Bell &amp; Drum Towers → Yongxing Fang food street</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Mount Hua</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Mount Hua (Huashan) day trip — one of China&#39;s five sacred mountains, famous plank walk</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">South Gate (Yongningmen) of the City Wall at night — fully illuminated and reflecting in the moat</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Terracotta Warriors Pit 1 from the elevated viewing platform at the far end — the classic panorama shot</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Muslim Quarter from the Drum Tower at dusk — red lanterns lighting up as the street comes alive</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Xi&#39;an is famous for replicas of the Terracotta Warriors in all sizes — from keychain miniatures to life-sized statues. The best selection and prices are at the factory shops near the Terracotta Warriors Museum, not in the city center. For antiques and curios: Shuyuanmen Street (behind the Forest of Steles Museum) has calligraphy brushes, ink stones, shadow puppets, and paper cuts — more authentic and less touristy than the Muslim Quarter shops. For local food products: the Muslim Quarter sells vacuum-packed lamb, cumin, dried persimmons, and locally made chili oil that makes a great souvenir.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Xi&#39;an&#39;s nightlife centers around three areas. The South Gate (Nanmen) area has the best concentration of bars — some built directly into the ancient wall&#39;s watchtowers, like the funky &#39;Defu Lane Bar Street.&#39; The Great Tang All Day Mall near the Giant Wild Goose Pagoda is a Tang-dynasty-themed pedestrian street that comes alive at night with costumed performers, light shows, and street food. For something cultural: the Tang Dynasty Music and Dance Show at the Shaanxi Grand Opera House combines ancient instruments and reconstructed Tang-era choreography. The Muslim Quarter after 10pm is quieter and more atmospheric — perfect for a late-night lamb skewer crawl.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$30–45/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$70–110/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$200+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Xi&#39;an City Wall Lantern Festival</span><span class="tag">Jan/Feb (CNY)</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">The South Gate section of the city wall is covered in massive illuminated lantern displays — dragons, phoenixes, and Silk Road themes. One of China&#39;s most spectacular lantern festivals.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Xi&#39;an Airport (XIY): Shuttle bus ¥25 (1h) or metro Line 14. High-speed rail to Beijing (4.5h), Chengdu (3h). Metro ¥2–8. Taxi ¥9 flagfall.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Xi&#39;an is very safe. Watch for pickpockets in the crowded Muslim Quarter and at the Terracotta Warriors (especially in Pit 1). The &#39;art student scam&#39; — where friendly English speakers invite you to see their &#39;art exhibition&#39; that turns into a high-pressure tea or painting sale — is common near the Drum Tower and South Gate. Taxis are plentiful but drivers rarely speak English. Didi is reliable and removes the language barrier. Air quality can be poor in winter.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="3" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Chengdu <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">成都</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Chengdu might be China&#39;s most livable big city. The capital of Sichuan province operates on a different rhythm than Beijing or Shanghai — slower, more indulgent, and unapologetically pleasure-seeking. This is where you spend mornings watching giant pandas munch bamboo, afternoons in a bamboo-chair tea house by the river having your ear professionally cleaned (a local tradition), and evenings gathered around a bubbling Sichuan hot pot that will reset your understanding of what &#39;spicy&#39; means. Chengdu&#39;s food culture is UNESCO-recognized as a City of Gastronomy, and the local philosophy of life — &#39;the best revenge is a life well-lived&#39; — permeates everything from the 24-hour hot pot joints to the mahjong games spilling onto sidewalks.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Pandas + food culture</span><span class="tag">3–4 days</span><span class="tag">Mar–Jun, Sep–Nov</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Food lovers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Animal lovers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Culture seekers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Those wanting a slower-paced China experience</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Lazybones Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Near Kuanzhai Alley &middot; $8–15/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">The Temple House</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Taikoo Li &middot; $120–200/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Niccolo Chengdu</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">IFS / Chunxi Road &middot; $200–350/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Sichuan Hot Pot</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Shujiuxiang (蜀九香)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">The classic Chengdu hot pot chain, expect a wait</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Mapo Tofu</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Chen Mapo Tofu (陈麻婆豆腐)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">The original since 1862, numbingly spicy</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Dan Dan Noodles</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Any good street stall (担担面)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Spicy sesame noodles, ¥10–15</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1.5h train</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Leshan Giant Buddha</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">71m carved Buddha, world&#39;s largest stone Buddha</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1h train</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Mount Qingcheng</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Taoist sacred mountain, lush forests</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1h train</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Dujiangyan</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">2,000-year-old irrigation system still in use</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Chengdu Research Base of Giant Panda Breeding <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">大熊猫繁育研究基地</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The world&#39;s premier panda conservation and breeding center, home to over 200 giant pandas living in a beautifully landscaped 100-hectare reserve that mimics their natural habitat. The highlight: the nursery and kindergarten areas where baby pandas (born July-September) tumble, play, and nurse — genuinely one of the most joyful wildlife experiences on earth. Red pandas, swans, and peacocks roam the grounds. Unlike a zoo, the pandas here have large naturalistic enclosures with bamboo forests, climbing structures, and pools.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥55</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">7:30–18:00 (last entry 17:00)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The pandas are most active 7:30–10:00am — that&#39;s when they&#39;re fed and the babies are at their most playful. After 10am they mostly sleep. Arrive at 7:30am sharp. The nursery area closes earlier than the main grounds — visit it first. Avoid weekends and Chinese holidays when crowds are intense. The base is 18km north of the city center — taxi or Didi takes 40 minutes (¥40–50).</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Jinli Ancient Street <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">锦里古街</span></h5></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A restored Qing-dynasty style pedestrian street adjacent to the Wuhou Shrine. At 350 meters long, it packs in traditional Sichuan architecture, red lanterns, teahouses, snack stalls, and craft shops. While thoroughly tourist-oriented, Jinli is genuinely charming — especially at night when the lanterns glow and the crowds thin out. It&#39;s adjacent to the Wuhou Shrine (dedicated to the Three Kingdoms-era strategist Zhuge Liang), which is worth visiting first for historical context.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free. Wuhou Shrine: ¥60</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Shops: 10:00–22:00. Street is always accessible</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go around 9pm on a weekday — the lanterns are lit, most tour groups have left, and the atmosphere is magical. The candy-blowing and sugar-painting stalls are fun to watch. For real, non-tourist street food, walk 15 minutes east to the Sichuan University area.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">People&#39;s Park &amp; Tea Houses <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">人民公园</span></h5></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The living room of Chengdu. This is where you understand the city&#39;s famous &#39;slow life&#39; philosophy. Heming Tea House, inside the park, has been serving tea since the 1920s under bamboo groves — order a cup of jasmine tea (¥15-30), and you can sit for hours while locals play mahjong, read newspapers, and have their ears cleaned by professional ear-pickers wielding an array of tiny metal tools. The park also has a bizarrely popular &#39;marriage market&#39; on weekends where parents post their adult children&#39;s resumes on umbrellas and trees in hopes of finding them a spouse.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Free entry. Tea: ¥15–30 per cup. Ear cleaning: ¥30–50</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">6:30–22:00 (park). Heming Tea House open roughly 8:00–19:00</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go on a weekday morning for the most authentic experience — you&#39;ll mainly see retired locals. The ear-cleaning experience is a Chengdu rite of passage: slightly terrifying but oddly satisfying. The matchmaking corner (weekend afternoons near the north gate) is peak Chengdu weirdness and highly photographable. Order a bowl of chuanchuan (skewered snacks) from vendors walking through the tea house.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>3 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Pandas!</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Chengdu Panda Base (7:30am, ¥55) → Jinli Ancient Street → Sichuan opera face-changing at Shufeng Yayun</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Culture &amp; Food</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Wuhou Shrine → People&#39;s Park tea house (Heming) → Kuanzhai Alley → Hot pot at Shujiuxiang (蜀九香)</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Leshan Buddha</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Leshan Giant Buddha day trip (1.5h train, ¥80) — 71m carved Buddha → evening Sichuan food tour</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>4 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Pandas</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Panda Base early → Sichuan Cuisine Museum (¥60) with cooking demo → Jinli night stroll</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">City Life</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">People&#39;s Park + tea → Wuhou Shrine → Kuanzhai Alley → Hot pot + Sichuan opera</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Leshan Buddha</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Leshan Giant Buddha day trip → evening street food at Jinli</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Dujiangyan</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Dujiangyan Irrigation (UNESCO, 1h train) — 2,000-year-old engineering, or Mount Qingcheng</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Panda Base nursery — baby pandas at feeding time (July-September for newborns)</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Jinli Street at night with all lanterns lit — use the bridge over the pond for the classic shot</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Heming Tea House in People&#39;s Park — elderly locals playing chess under bamboo with tea steam rising</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Chengdu&#39;s shopping is more about food than fashion. For Sichuan peppercorns and chili products: the spice markets near the North Railway Station sell every variety of dried chili and the prized &#39;flower pepper&#39; (huajiao) that produces Sichuan cuisine&#39;s signature numbing sensation. For traditional crafts: Jinli Street and Kuanzhai Alley have Shu embroidery (one of China&#39;s four great embroidery traditions), bamboo weaving, and lacquerware. For modern shopping: Taikoo Li — an open-air luxury shopping complex next to the 1,600-year-old Daci Temple, where Prada and Gucci sit alongside a Tang dynasty pagoda. For panda merchandise: the Panda Base gift shop has the most variety and quality, with proceeds supporting conservation.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Chengdu&#39;s nightlife is inseparable from its food culture. The quintessential evening: a Sichuan hot pot dinner at a restaurant like Shujiuxiang (蜀九香) where you cook thinly sliced meats, tofu, and vegetables in a bubbling cauldron of chili oil and Sichuan peppercorns, washing it down with cold beer. After dinner: a Sichuan opera performance at Shufeng Yayun Teahouse — the highlight is &#39;face-changing&#39; (bian lian), where performers instantly switch colorful masks with a flick of the head, a closely guarded trade secret. For drinks: the Jiuyanqiao area (Nine-Eye Bridge) has riverside bars and craft beer spots. The Lan Kwai Fong Chengdu complex attracts an international crowd. For something uniquely local: find a late-night street-side barbecue (shaokao) stall and join locals at plastic tables eating grilled skewers until 3am.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$25–40/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$60–100/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$200+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Chengdu International Panda Festival</span><span class="tag">Sep/Oct</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">Celebrations around panda conservation with special exhibits at the Panda Base, panda-themed art installations, and cultural performances.</p></div><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Lantern Festival</span><span class="tag">Feb</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">Jinli Street and Wuhou Shrine host elaborate lantern displays. Unlike northern China&#39;s lantern festivals, Chengdu&#39;s incorporate Sichuan folk motifs and local craftsmanship.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Tianfu (TFU): Metro Line 18 (¥10, 50 min). Shuangliu (CTU): Metro Line 10 (¥5). Metro ¥2–10. High-speed rail to Xi&#39;an (3h), Chongqing (1.5h).</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Chengdu is very safe. The biggest risk is overestimating your spice tolerance — Sichuan peppercorns produce a numbing sensation (ma) that&#39;s different from capsaicin heat (la) and can be overwhelming. Start with mild spice levels and work up. Be aware of the &#39;tea ceremony scam&#39; near tourist areas where friendly strangers invite you to a tea experience that ends with an inflated bill. Tap water is not drinkable. Summers are hot and humid; the city is in a basin that traps moisture and pollution. Winters are mild but overcast — Chengdu is one of China&#39;s cloudiest cities.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="4" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Zhangjiajie <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">张家界</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Zhangjiajie is China&#39;s most surreal landscape — a forest of 3,000 quartz-sandstone pillars rising through mist like the floating mountains in James Cameron&#39;s Avatar (which they directly inspired). Located in northwestern Hunan province, this UNESCO Global Geopark is a place where geology has gone gloriously wrong: ancient seabeds were pushed skyward, then carved by millennia of erosion into knife-edge columns draped in pine trees and wreathed in cloud. The park spans 480 hectares within the larger Wulingyuan Scenic Area, which also includes the world&#39;s highest outdoor elevator (326m Bailong Elevator built into a cliff face) and the world&#39;s longest and highest glass-bottomed bridge (430m across Zhangjiajie Grand Canyon).</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Nature + adventure</span><span class="tag">2–4 days</span><span class="tag">Apr–May, Sep–Oct</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Nature lovers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Adventure travelers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Photographers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Avatar fans</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhangjiajie Zhongtian Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Wulingyuan entrance &middot; $8–15/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Pullman Zhangjiajie</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Wulingyuan &middot; $80–130/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Neodalle Zhangjiajie</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Wulingyuan &middot; $150–250/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Tujia Three-pot Stew</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Wulingyuan local restaurants (三下锅)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Hearty Tujia minority dish with pork, tofu, vegetables</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Sour Fish Hot Pot</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Park entrance area (酸汤鱼)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Tujia-style sour and spicy fish stew</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">3h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Fenghuang Ancient Town</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Stilt houses along Tuojiang River at night</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhangjiajie National Forest Park <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">张家界国家森林公园</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">China&#39;s first national forest park and the core of the Wulingyuan UNESCO site. The park contains over 3,000 quartz-sandstone pillars, some reaching 200 meters, connected by a network of paved trails, shuttle buses, and cable cars. Key areas: Yuanjiajie (the &#39;Avatar Mountains&#39; with the Southern Sky Column — renamed &#39;Avatar Hallelujah Mountain&#39; after the film), Golden Whip Stream (a 7.5km flat walk along a crystal-clear creek through the canyon floor), and Tianzi Mountain (the highest area with the most dramatic panoramic views).</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥225 (4-day pass, includes park shuttle buses). Cable cars: ¥65–76 each</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">7:00–18:00 (varies by season)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Buy the 4-day pass — trying to see everything in one day is impossible and miserable. Get to the park entrance by 7am to beat tour groups. The cable cars and Bailong Elevator have separate tickets not included in the park pass. Wulingyuan is the main entry town with the best accommodation and restaurant options.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhangjiajie Grand Canyon Glass Bridge <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">张家界大峡谷玻璃桥</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">At 430 meters long and suspended 300 meters above the canyon floor, this was the world&#39;s longest and highest glass-bottomed bridge when it opened in 2016. Walking across the transparent panels feels genuinely disorienting — you can see the canyon floor through your feet. The bridge also features the world&#39;s highest bungee jump (260m) for the truly fearless. After crossing, a series of paths, waterfalls, and a boat ride take you through the canyon below.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥256 (bridge + canyon). Bungee jump: ¥2,998</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:00–17:00</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Book online in advance — daily visitor numbers are capped. No cameras or phones are allowed on the bridge (lockers provided) — you can pay for an official photo. Go on a clear day; fog obscures the canyon view. The combined bridge + canyon route involves many steps descending into the gorge — moderate fitness required.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>2 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Avatar Mountains</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">National Forest Park (¥225) → Yuanjiajie (Avatar Mountain) → Bailong Elevator → Tianzi Mountain</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Glass Bridge</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Grand Canyon Glass Bridge (¥256) → Tianmen Mountain (¥258) — Heaven&#39;s Gate and 99-bend road</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>4 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Forest Park</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Golden Whip Stream → Huangshi Village → Yuanjiajie scenic area</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Tianzi Mountain</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tianzi Mountain sunrise → Bailong Elevator → Ten-mile Gallery → Wulingyuan evening</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Glass Bridge</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Grand Canyon + Glass Bridge → Baofeng Lake boat ride</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Tianmen Mountain</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tianmen Mountain cable car → Heaven&#39;s Gate → 99-bend road → cliff walk</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Yuanjiajie viewing platform looking toward the Southern Sky Column — come at sunrise for mist swirling between pillars</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tianzi Mountain summit at sunset — the &#39;Western Sea&#39; of stone peaks in golden light</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Glass Bridge at midday when the light through the glass floor creates dramatic shadow patterns on the canyon walls</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Zhangjiajie shopping is limited. The main souvenirs are Tujia minority crafts: brocade textiles with geometric patterns, silver jewelry, and wood carvings. The shops along Wulingyuan&#39;s main street have the best selection. Local specialties to buy: Zhangjiajie berry tea (a caffeine-free herbal tea made from local vine leaves), and dried kiwi fruit — the region grows exceptional kiwis. Avoid the &#39;jade&#39; and &#39;antique&#39; shops in tourist areas — almost all are fakes.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Zhangjiajie&#39;s nightlife is subdued — this is a nature destination, not a party town. Wulingyuan town has a cluster of restaurants and a few bars along the main street. The &#39;Charming Xiangxi&#39; cultural show (¥228) is a large-scale outdoor performance showcasing Tujia and Miao minority music, dance, and folklore — touristy but entertaining, with impressive acrobatics and light effects. Evening activities are mostly about recovering from hiking: foot massages (widely available and cheap after a day on your feet), and trying local dishes at family-run restaurants.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$30–50/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$70–120/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$200+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhangjiajie International Country Music Week</span><span class="tag">Sep</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">Folk musicians from around the world perform in the park — an unusual cultural overlay in this natural setting.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Zhangjiajie Airport (DYG): Taxi to Wulingyuan (¥100, 40 min). High-speed rail from Changsha (3h), Chongqing (4h). Park shuttles included in ticket.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">The main risks are physical: steep stairs, slippery paths after rain, and vertigo on glass platforms and cliff walks. Wear proper hiking shoes, not sandals. Stay on marked paths — venturing off can lead to unstable cliff edges. Monkeys in the park are aggressive about food — don&#39;t eat openly near them and secure your bags. July-August brings heavy rain and occasional trail closures; April-May and September-October are ideal. Tianmen Mountain&#39;s glass cliff walk (separate from the Grand Canyon bridge) can close in high winds.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="5" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Guilin &amp; Yangshuo <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">桂林/阳朔</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Guilin and Yangshuo deliver the China of imagination: limestone karst peaks rising vertically from emerald rice paddies, water buffalo cooling in rivers, and cormorant fishermen working by lantern light. This landscape — immortalized in classical Chinese painting and poetry for over a thousand years — is the single most photographed scenery in China. The Li River corridor between Guilin and Yangshuo is an 83km gallery of karst formations, each with poetic names like &#39;Nine Horses Fresco Hill&#39; and &#39;Yellow Cloth Reflection.&#39; Yangshuo, the small town at the river&#39;s end, has evolved from a sleepy backpacker haven into China&#39;s outdoor adventure capital — world-class rock climbing on limestone crags, cycling through village lanes, bamboo rafting on the Yulong River, and some of the best-value boutique hotels in Asia.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Karst scenery + cycling</span><span class="tag">3–4 days</span><span class="tag">Apr–Oct</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Outdoor enthusiasts</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Photographers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Couples</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Backpackers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Cyclists</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yangshuo Sudder Street Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">West Street area &middot; $8–15/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yangshuo Mountain Retreat</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Yulong River &middot; $60–120/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Banyan Tree Yangshuo</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Fuli Town &middot; $300+/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Guilin Rice Noodles</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Chongshan Rice Noodles (崇善米粉)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">City signature breakfast, ¥10–15 with all toppings</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Beer Fish</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">West Street riverside restaurants (啤酒鱼)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Yangshuo specialty — river fish braised in beer</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2h from Guilin</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Longji Rice Terraces</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Dragon&#39;s Backbone terraces, best May–Oct</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">40 min from Yangshuo</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Xingping Ancient Town</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The view on the ¥20 note</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Li River Cruise <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">漓江游船</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">The quintessential China experience. Four hours drifting down the Li River from Guilin to Yangshuo through a landscape that has defined Chinese aesthetics for millennia. The cruise passes bamboo groves, water buffalo, villages clinging to riverbanks, and the iconic karst peaks that appear on the ¥20 Chinese banknote. The scenery is slow and hypnotic — every bend reveals a new composition of peaks, water, and sky.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥360–420 (3-4 star boat, includes lunch). ¥215 budget option</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Departures 9:00–10:00am from Guilin docks. Arrival in Yangshuo ~1:30pm</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The 4-star boat (¥420) has a better lunch buffet, fewer passengers, and more comfortable seating — worth the upgrade. Sit on the upper deck for the best views. Bring sunglasses and sunscreen — you&#39;ll be on deck for hours. The budget bamboo raft option covers only part of the route but gets you closer to the water. Book through your hotel or Trip.com — official ticket office prices are fixed.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yangshuo Countryside &amp; Yulong River <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">阳朔乡村 &amp; 遇龙河</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Yangshuo&#39;s countryside is best explored by bicycle or electric scooter. The &#39;Ten-Mile Gallery&#39; road runs along the Yulong River past karst peaks, organic farms, and villages where life hasn&#39;t changed much in decades. The Yulong River bamboo rafts (smaller and more intimate than the Li River boats) take you on a 40-90 minute drift through the most concentrated karst scenery, poled by a boatman in traditional style. Moon Hill — a karst peak with a natural moon-shaped arch carved through it — offers a short but steep 30-minute climb to one of the best viewpoints in the region.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bike rental: ¥20–40/day. Bamboo raft: ¥160–255 depending on route section. Moon Hill: ¥15</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Daylight hours for cycling. Bamboo rafts: 7:30–17:00 (last departure 16:00)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Rent an electric scooter (¥50-80/day) if you can — the countryside is larger than it looks and summer heat is intense. The Yulong River bamboo raft routes sell out on weekends — book ahead. For Moon Hill, go at sunrise to have the summit to yourself. Water Buffalo Valley (upstream Yulong) is less touristy than the main rafting sections.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>3 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Li River Cruise</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Guilin → Yangshuo cruise (4h, ¥360) → West Street → Impression Liu Sanjie show</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Yangshuo Countryside</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bike Ten-Mile Gallery → Yulong River bamboo raft (¥200) → Moon Hill hike → Farmhouse dinner</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Rice Terraces</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Longji Rice Terraces day trip (2h, ¥80) — hike between Ping&#39;an and Dazhai villages</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>4 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Arrive Guilin</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Elephant Trunk Hill → Reed Flute Cave → Sun &amp; Moon Pagodas at night</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Li River</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Li River cruise to Yangshuo → explore West Street afternoon</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Yangshuo</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Morning cycling → Yulong River rafting → Moon Hill → evening cooking class</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Longji Terraces</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Longji Rice Terraces day trip — Dazhai village hike, Yao minority culture</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Xingping Town — the exact spot pictured on the ¥20 banknote; go to the riverbank at dawn for the classic shot with a fisherman and cormorants</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Xianggong Hill — a 20-minute climb to a viewing pavilion overlooking the Li River&#39;s most dramatic bend with karst peaks receding into haze</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Yulong River bridge at sunrise — mist rising from the river with bamboo rafts drifting past karst silhouettes</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Yangshuo&#39;s West Street is tourist-oriented but fun for an evening stroll. Local specialties: Yangshuo painted fans (hand-painted with local landscapes), Guilin Sanhua rice wine (a clear spirit aged in clay pots), and osmanthus-scented products (tea, candy, honey) from the region&#39;s osmanthus trees. The Yangshuo Painting and Calligraphy Street (off Pantao Road) has artists who will paint personalized scrolls on the spot. For outdoor gear: several shops on West Street sell climbing equipment and outdoor clothing — useful if you need replacement gear.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Yangshuo&#39;s West Street transforms into a lively bar and restaurant strip after dark. It&#39;s backpacker-oriented — think cheap beers, live cover bands, and rooftop terraces with karst views. For something more refined: rooftop cocktails at one of the boutique hotels along the Yulong River. The &#39;Impression Liu Sanjie&#39; show (¥198–680) is a massive outdoor water-light performance directed by Zhang Yimou (who directed the 2008 Beijing Olympics opening ceremony), staged on a 2km stretch of the Li River with 600 performers — the karst peaks themselves are the backdrop and are lit up as part of the performance.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$30–45/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$70–110/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$250+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yangshuo Rock Climbing Festival</span><span class="tag">Oct/Nov</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">International climbing community gathers for competitions, workshops, and new route development on Yangshuo&#39;s world-class limestone crags.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Guilin Airport (KWL): Shuttle ¥20 (40 min). High-speed rail to Guangzhou (2.5h), Chengdu (5h). Yangshuo is 1.5h by bus from Guilin (¥25).</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">The main risks are outdoor activity-related: wear helmets when cycling on main roads (traffic can be chaotic), bring sufficient water for bike trips in summer, and don&#39;t swim in the Li River (strong currents in places). Rock climbing should only be done with licensed operators. Bamboo rafts are generally safe but wear the provided life jacket. Mosquitoes can be intense in summer — bring repellent. The summer heat (June-August) is oppressive for outdoor activities; April-May and September-November are ideal.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="6" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Yunnan <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">云南</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Yunnan is China condensed into one province — snow-capped Tibetan peaks in the north, tropical rainforests on the Myanmar border, and everything in between. This is China&#39;s most ethnically diverse province: 25 of China&#39;s 55 officially recognized ethnic minorities live here, each with distinct languages, clothing, festivals, and cuisines. The classic Yunnan circuit — Kunming, Dali, Lijiang, Shangri-La — traces the ancient Tea Horse Road, a trading network that predates the Silk Road. Along this route, you&#39;ll find Stone Forest&#39;s petrified trees turned to karst, Erhai Lake&#39;s cycling paths through Bai minority villages, Lijiang&#39;s UNESCO-listed maze of cobblestone lanes and wooden bridges, and Tiger Leaping Gorge — one of the deepest river canyons on earth with a legendary 2-day hiking trail.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Culture + diverse scenery</span><span class="tag">5–7 days</span><span class="tag">Mar–Jun, Sep–Nov</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Hikers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Culture seekers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Photographers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Solo travelers</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Ethnic minority enthusiasts</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Kunming Cloudland Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Kunming city center &middot; $8–15/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">The Bivou Lijiang</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Lijiang Old Town &middot; $60–120/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Amandayan Lijiang</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Lijiang Old Town &middot; $500+/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Crossing-the-Bridge Noodles</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Jianxin Yuan (建新园)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Yunnan&#39;s most famous dish, Kunming specialty</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yak Hot Pot</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Shangri-La local spots (牦牛火锅)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">High-altitude Tibetan comfort food</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Er Kuai (Rice Cake)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Dali street stalls (饵块)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Grilled rice cakes with savory fillings</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">1.5h from Kunming</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Stone Forest</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">UNESCO karst formation, otherworldly rock landscape</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2h from Lijiang</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Shaxi Ancient Town</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Well-preserved Tea Horse Road town</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Lijiang Old Town <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">丽江古城</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A UNESCO World Heritage site and one of the best-preserved ancient towns in China. The Naxi minority built this warren of cobblestone streets, wooden buildings, stone bridges, and clear streams fed by melting snow from Jade Dragon Snow Mountain over 800 years ago. The town is crisscrossed by canals — a waterwheel system that has functioned since the Song dynasty — and every corner reveals another courtyard garden or lattice-window facade. At night, red lanterns illuminate the lanes and bars feature live Naxi music (a unique fusion of Taoist ritual music with folk traditions).</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥50 (maintenance fee, valid for multiple entries over 7 days)</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Always open. Attractions within the old town generally 8:30–17:30</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Stay inside the old town — the morning hours before 9am, when only residents are awake and the cobblestones are wet from being washed, are the most magical. The town gets extremely crowded 10am-6pm. For a quieter alternative, Shuhe Ancient Town (15 minutes north) is like Lijiang 20 years ago — smaller, less commercial, and genuinely lived-in.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Tiger Leaping Gorge <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">虎跳峡</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">One of the deepest river gorges in the world — the Jinsha River (upper Yangtze) squeezes between Jade Dragon Snow Mountain (5,596m) and Haba Snow Mountain (5,396m), carving a canyon that plunges 3,790 meters from peak to river. The 2-day hiking trail along the gorge&#39;s upper rim is one of the world&#39;s classic treks: well-marked, moderately challenging, and consistently jaw-dropping as the snow peaks reveal themselves through the clouds. Guesthouses along the trail (Naxi Guesthouse, Halfway Guesthouse, Tina&#39;s) offer simple rooms and hot meals with views that would cost $500/night anywhere else.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥65 entrance fee</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Daylight hours for hiking</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">The hike is best done over 2 days with an overnight at Halfway Guesthouse (the balcony view of Jade Dragon Snow Mountain at sunset is legendary). Hike south to north (Qiaotou → Tina&#39;s) for the classic experience. The trail is well-maintained but has sections with steep drop-offs — not recommended for those with vertigo. April-May and September-October are ideal; avoid July-August rainy season when landslides are possible. No special gear needed beyond good hiking shoes.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>5 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Kunming Arrival</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Green Lake Park → Yuantong Temple → Nanqiang Street for crossing-the-bridge noodles</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Dali Old Town</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">High-speed rail to Dali (2h) → Old Town → Erhai Lake cycling → Three Pagodas</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Lijiang</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Train to Lijiang (1.5h) → UNESCO Old Town → Black Dragon Pool → Naxi music performance</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Tiger Leaping Gorge</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Gorge hike day 1 — Qiaotou → Naxi Guesthouse halfway (6–7h hiking)</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 5</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Gorge Finish</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Halfway → Tina&#39;s → Middle Gorge → return to Lijiang</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>7 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Kunming</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Stone Forest day trip (1.5h, ¥175) → Green Lake Park evening</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Dali</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Train to Dali → Erhai Lake cycling → Xizhou Old Town → tie-dye workshop</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Dali → Lijiang</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Cangshan Mountain cable car → train to Lijiang → Old Town evening</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 4</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Gorge Day 1</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Tiger Leaping Gorge — Qiaotou to Halfway Guesthouse</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 5</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Gorge Day 2</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Finish hike → return to Lijiang → Shuhe Ancient Town</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 6</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Shangri-La</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Bus to Shangri-La (4h) → Dukezong Ancient Town → Songzanlin Monastery (¥115)</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 7</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Return</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Pudacuo National Park → fly out from Diqing or return to Lijiang</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Halfway Guesthouse balcony at sunset — Jade Dragon Snow Mountain&#39;s north face glowing gold</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Lijiang Old Town from Wangu Tower at dawn — the sea of grey-tiled roofs with mountains behind</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Erhai Lake from the eastern shore road — traditional fishing boats with Cangshan Mountain backdrop</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Yunnan&#39;s craft traditions are among China&#39;s richest. In Dali: Bai minority tie-dye (a indigo-blue fabric art), silver jewelry, and marble from Cangshan Mountain carved into decorative pieces. In Lijiang: Naxi Dongba pictographic script items — the only pictographic writing system still in use, printed on scrolls, t-shirts, and wooden plaques. Tibetan areas around Shangri-La: thangka paintings (religious scroll art), yak wool textiles, and Tibetan incense. The Dali Old Town Foreigner Street has the best concentration of craft shops. Yunnan is also China&#39;s premier tea region — pu&#39;er tea (aged, fermented tea cakes) from Xishuangbanna and Dian Hong (Yunnan black tea) are the specialties to buy.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Yunnan&#39;s nightlife varies dramatically by town. Lijiang: the old town has a famous bar street with live music — Naxi musicians playing traditional instruments alongside cover bands. Some bars double as cultural spaces where you can learn about Dongba religion and Naxi pictographs. Dali: more laid-back, with lake-view bars along Erhai&#39;s shore and courtyard cafés in the old town where travelers and expats gather. Shangri-La: the smallest scene, focused around Tibetan-style bars with butter lamps and yak-bone decorations where you might catch an impromptu Tibetan folk music performance.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$30–50/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$60–110/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$200+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Torch Festival (Yi minority)</span><span class="tag">Jul/Aug</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">Massive bonfires, torch parades, wrestling competitions, and all-night dancing in Yi communities throughout Yunnan. Kunming and Dali have the largest celebrations.</p></div><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Water-Splashing Festival (Dai minority)</span><span class="tag">Apr</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">Yunnan&#39;s version of Songkran — everyone gets soaked in Xishuangbanna and other Dai areas. Expect water fights, dragon boat races, and releasing floating lanterns at dusk.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Kunming Airport (KMG): Metro Line 6 (¥5, 30 min). High-speed rail: Kunming → Dali (2h) → Lijiang (1.5h). Bus Lijiang → Shangri-La (4h).</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Altitude is the main concern — Lijiang is at 2,400m and Shangri-La at 3,200m. Spend a day acclimatizing in Lijiang before heading higher. Tiger Leaping Gorge hiking requires reasonable fitness; the trail is safe but has unfenced drop-offs. In Tibetan areas, respect local customs: walk clockwise around temples and stupas, don&#39;t touch anyone&#39;s head, and ask before photographing monks. The mountain roads in Yunnan are winding — take motion sickness medication for bus rides. Tap water is not drinkable anywhere in the province.</p></div></div></div></div><div data-tab="7" class="dest-tab-panel"><div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-8"><div><h3 class="heading-md text-2xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Guangzhou <span class="text-lg text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">广州</span></h3><p class="body-text text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">Guangzhou is China&#39;s greatest food city — a claim Cantonese people make without hesitation and with good reason. As the capital of Guangdong province and the historic heart of Cantonese culture, Guangzhou has been a trading port for over 2,000 years and the starting point of the Maritime Silk Road. This mercantile history created a cuisine obsessed with freshness, technique, and the natural flavor of ingredients — dim sum, roasted meats, and seafood are elevated to art forms here. Beyond food, Guangzhou offers a fascinating contrast: the gleaming Canton Tower and Zhujiang New Town skyline on one side, and the narrow lanes of the old Liwan district where traditional herbal medicine shops, mahjong parlors, and family-run wonton noodle stalls have operated for generations on the other.</p><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-4"><span class="tag">Cantonese food + trade hub</span><span class="tag">2–3 days</span><span class="tag">Oct–Dec</span></div><div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mb-6"><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Food travelers (priority #1)</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Shopping</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Southern China gateway</span><span class="tag bg-blue-50/70 text-blue-700 border-blue-200">Architecture enthusiasts</span></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mb-8"><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Where to Stay</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Budget</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Lazy Gaga Hostel</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Yuexiu / Beijing Road &middot; $10–18/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Mid-range</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">LN Hotel Five</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Haizhu / Pearl River &middot; $80–150/night</p></div><div><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2"><span class="tag">Luxury</span><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Four Seasons Guangzhou</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Zhujiang New Town &middot; $250–400/night</p></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Must-Try Food</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Dim Sum</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Panxi Restaurant (泮溪酒家)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Classic garden restaurant, go before 10am for full cart service</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">White Cut Chicken</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Wenji (文记壹心鸡)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">The definitive Cantonese chicken dish</div></div><div><div class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Wonton Noodles</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)]">Baozai Wonton Noodles (宝仔云吞面)</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-0.5">Shrimp wontons with thin egg noodles in clear broth</div></div></div></div><div class="card p-5"><h4 class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nearby Day Trips</h4><div class="space-y-2"><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">2h drive</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Kaiping Diaolou</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">UNESCO fortified watchtowers, unique architectural fusion</div></div></div><div class="flex items-start gap-3"><span class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-text-muted)] w-20 flex-shrink-0">30 min by metro</span><div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)]">Foshan</div><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Ancestral Temple, martial arts heritage</div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Top Attractions</h4><div class="space-y-4"><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Chen Clan Academy <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">陈家祠</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">A stunning complex built in 1894 as both a family temple and an academy for Chen clan members preparing for imperial examinations. Now a folk art museum, every surface — roofs, beams, walls, screens — is covered in exquisite examples of traditional Cantonese craftsmanship: ceramic ridgepole figurines depicting historical dramas, intricate wood carvings, stone sculptures, and ironwork. The level of decorative detail is staggering and provides a crash course in Guangdong&#39;s artistic heritage in one building.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">¥10</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">8:30–17:30</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Don&#39;t rush — the details emerge slowly. Look up at the roof ridges for the ceramic figurine scenes. The museum shop sells quality reproductions of Guangdong crafts at reasonable prices. Combine with a visit to the nearby Liwan district for authentic old Guangzhou atmosphere.</span></div></div><div class="card p-5"><div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2"><h5 class="text-base font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Canton Tower &amp; Zhujiang New Town <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">广州塔 &amp; 珠江新城</span></h5><span class="text-xs text-amber-500 font-bold">&#9733; Must See</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Canton Tower (600m) was the world&#39;s tallest tower when completed in 2010 — a twisting hyperboloid structure nicknamed &#39;Slim Waist&#39; by locals. The observation deck at 488 meters offers panoramic views across the Pearl River Delta. Across the river, Zhujiang New Town is Guangzhou&#39;s CBD: the Guangdong Museum (a striking black-and-red puzzle-box building by Rocco Design, free entry), the Guangzhou Opera House (Zaha Hadid&#39;s fluid twin-boulder design, one of her masterpieces), and the IFC tower. The area around Huacheng Square is particularly impressive at night when the towers put on coordinated LED light shows.</p><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2 mb-2 text-xs"><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Price:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canton Tower: ¥150 (indoor observation), ¥228 (outdoor observation). Guangdong Museum: free</span></div><div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Hours:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canton Tower: 9:30–22:30. Guangdong Museum: 9:00–17:00 (closed Mondays)</span></div></div><div class="bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 text-xs"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">Tips:</span> <span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Go up Canton Tower around 5pm to see Guangzhou in daylight, at sunset, and lit up at night. The outdoor observation deck (not the indoor one) is worth the extra cost. The &#39;Bubble Tram&#39; that circles the tower&#39;s rim is a separate ticket and a unique experience. Guangdong Museum requires online reservation — book 1-3 days ahead on their WeChat mini-program.</span></div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Day-by-Day Itineraries</h4><div class="space-y-4"><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>2 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Culture &amp; Dim Sum</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Dim sum at Panxi (泮溪酒家) → Chen Clan Academy (¥10) → Shamian Island → Pearl River night cruise</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Modern Guangzhou</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canton Tower (¥150) → Guangdong Museum (free) → Beijing Road → Shangxiajiu food street</div></div></div></details><details class="card p-4 cursor-pointer"><summary class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] list-none flex items-center justify-between"><span>3 Days Suggested Plan</span><span class="summary-arrow text-xs">[+]</span></summary><div class="mt-3 space-y-3 pt-3 border-t border-[var(--color-border-light)]"><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 1</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Old Guangzhou</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Dim sum → Chen Clan Academy → Shamian Island → Qingping Market → Baietan evening</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 2</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">New Guangzhou</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canton Tower → Zhujiang New Town → Guangdong Museum → Huacheng Square light show</div></div><div class="pl-3 border-l-2 border-[var(--color-accent-light)]"><div class="text-xs font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)] mb-1">Day 3</div><div class="text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-text)] mb-0.5">Day Trip</div><div class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Kaiping Diaolou (UNESCO, 2h) or Foshan (Nanfeng Kiln, martial arts museum, 30 min metro)</div></div></div></details></div></div><div class="mb-8 card p-5"><h4 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Best Photo Spots</h4><ul class="space-y-2"><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Canton Tower at night from the Haixinsha Island pedestrian bridge — the tower reflected in the Pearl River</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Shamian Island at golden hour — European colonial buildings under banyan trees, former British and French concessions frozen in time</span></li><li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-sm"><span class="text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-0.5">&#8226;</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]">Chen Clan Academy roof ridge — zoom in on the ceramic figurines for the most photogenic architectural details in Guangzhou</span></li></ul></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Shopping Guide</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Guangzhou is a wholesale capital — the scale of its markets is hard to overstate. For fashion: the Zhanxi Road garment wholesale district (largest in China) and Baima Clothing Market have everything from fast fashion to designer samples at wholesale prices if you buy in bulk (or bargain hard for single items). For leather goods: Sanyuanli Leather Market has bags, belts, and wallets. For electronics: the Gangding computer markets near South China Normal University. For traditional goods: Shangxiajiu Pedestrian Street in the old Liwan district has Cantonese pastry shops, silk stores, and herbal medicine dispensaries dating back a century. For upscale shopping: Taikoo Hui and Grandview Mall.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Nightlife</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Guangzhou&#39;s nightlife revolves around eating — the city has more restaurants per capita than any other in China. The Shangxiajiu and Beijing Road areas have late-night food streets where stalls serve claypot rice, roasted goose, and seafood until the early hours. For bars: the Party Pier area along the Pearl River has club-style venues with river views. The Tianhe district around Zhujiang New Town has sophisticated cocktail bars — Hope and Sesame and Spin are craft cocktail institutions. For a uniquely Guangzhou evening: take a Pearl River night cruise past the illuminated Canton Tower and Zhujiang New Town skyline (¥55–100, departures until 10pm from Dashatou Pier).</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">City Budget (Per Person / Day)</h4><div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4"><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Budget</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$30–50/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Mid-range</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$80–130/day</div></div><div class="card p-4 text-center"><div class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mb-1">Luxury</div><div class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">$250+/day</div></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Seasonal Events &amp; Festivals</h4><div class="space-y-3"><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Canton Fair</span><span class="tag">Apr &amp; Oct</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">China&#39;s largest trade fair, held biannually since 1957. Hotel prices triple across the city — avoid Guangzhou during the Fair unless you&#39;re attending for business.</p></div><div class="card p-4"><div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-1"><span class="text-sm font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Guangzhou International Light Festival</span><span class="tag">Nov</span></div><p class="body-text text-sm">The Zhujiang New Town skyline and Huacheng Square become a canvas for massive light installations and projection mappings.</p></div></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Getting There &amp; Around</h4><div class="card p-5"><p class="body-text text-sm">Baiyun Airport (CAN): Metro Line 3 to city (¥8, 45 min). High-speed rail to Shenzhen (30 min), Hong Kong (1h), Guilin (2.5h). Metro ¥2–12, 16 lines.</p></div></div><div class="mb-8"><h4 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Safety Tips</h4><div class="card p-5 bg-amber-50/30 border-amber-100"><p class="body-text text-sm">Guangzhou is very safe. The main annoyance is aggressive touts at the train station and around the wholesale markets. Watch your belongings in crowded shopping districts. Summers (May-September) are oppressively hot and humid with frequent thunderstorms — carry an umbrella at all times. Typhoon season runs August-October, though direct hits on Guangzhou are rare. Tap water is not drinkable. English is less widely spoken than in Beijing or Shanghai — have your destinations written in Chinese characters.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></section><section id="accommodation" class="section-alt"><div class="container"><div class="max-w-3xl mb-10 reveal"><span class="section-eyebrow">Where to Stay</span><h2 class="heading-lg text-3xl md:text-4xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">How to Find the Right Hotel in China</h2><p class="body-text text-lg">From hostels to five-star heritage properties, here is what to expect at each budget level and how to book.</p></div><div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 stagger-children reveal"><div class="card p-6"><h3 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Budget</h3><div class="tag mb-3">$10-30/night</div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Clean hostels and guesthouses near major sights. English-speaking staff, communal areas.</p><div class="text-xs bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 mb-3"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">How to book:</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]"> Book on Hostelworld or Trip.com. Look for 4.0+ rating. Many offer free luggage storage.</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Solo travelers, backpackers, students</p></div><div class="card p-6"><h3 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Mid-range</h3><div class="tag mb-3">$50-150/night</div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">Comfortable 3-4 star hotels or boutique guesthouses. Western amenities, metro access, breakfast included.</p><div class="text-xs bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 mb-3"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">How to book:</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]"> Trip.com has the best inventory. Agoda for international chains. Book 2-4 weeks ahead.</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Couples, families, first-time visitors</p></div><div class="card p-6"><h3 class="heading-md text-xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-2">Luxury</h3><div class="tag mb-3">$150-500+/night</div><p class="body-text text-sm mb-3">5-star international chains and heritage properties like converted hutong courtyard homes in Beijing.</p><div class="text-xs bg-[var(--color-accent-light)] rounded-lg p-3 mb-3"><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-accent)]">How to book:</span><span class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)]"> Book directly for best rates and perks. Many offer airport pickup.</span></div><p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Honeymooners, special occasions, comfort seekers</p></div></div><div class="max-w-3xl mt-8 card p-5 reveal reveal-delay-1"><p class="body-text text-sm"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> For each city guide in the Destinations section above, we list specific recommended hotels at each budget level. Scroll up and click through each city tab to find where to stay in Beijing, Shanghai, Xi'an, Chengdu, Zhangjiajie, Guilin/Yangshuo, Yunnan, and Guangzhou.</p></div><div class="max-w-3xl mt-4 card p-5 reveal"><p class="body-text text-sm"><strong>Important for foreign visitors:</strong> China only allows hotels with a foreign-stay license (涉外酒店) to host international guests. All international chains and every property we recommend hold this license, but double-check before booking in smaller towns or family-run guesthouses — some homestays cannot legally check you in.</p></div></div></section>

    <section class="section-alt">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="max-w-3xl mb-8 reveal">
          <span class="section-eyebrow">Compare</span>
          <h2 class="heading-lg text-3xl md:text-4xl text-[var(--color-text)] mb-4">Which City Is Right for You?</h2>
          <p class="body-text text-lg">Can't decide between two cities? Here is what makes each one unique.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-5 stagger-children reveal">
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Beijing <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">vs</span> Shanghai</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs mb-3">
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Beijing</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Ancient capital, Great Wall, hutongs, imperial history. Slower pace, colder winters. Best for first-timers who love history.</p></div>
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Shanghai</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Futuristic skyline, Art Deco streets, world-class dining. Fast-paced, cosmopolitan. Best for city lovers and foodies.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)] italic">Verdict: Do both. Beijing is the historical heart; Shanghai is China's future. They complement, not compete.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Guilin <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">vs</span> Zhangjiajie</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs mb-3">
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Guilin & Yangshuo</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Gentle karst peaks, Li River cruise, cycling through rice paddies. Relaxed, photogenic, great for couples.</p></div>
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Zhangjiajie</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Towering sandstone pillars, glass bridges, world's highest elevator. Dramatic, adventurous, Avatar-like scenery.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)] italic">Verdict: Guilin for relaxation and romance. Zhangjiajie for adrenaline and jaw-drop moments.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Chengdu <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">vs</span> Xi'an</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs mb-3">
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Chengdu</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Pandas, fiery Sichuan food, tea house culture, laid-back lifestyle. Gateway to Tibet and Yunnan.</p></div>
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Xi'an</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Terracotta Warriors, ancient city wall biking, Muslim Quarter street food. The starting point of the Silk Road.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)] italic">Verdict: Chengdu for food and pandas. Xi'an for ancient history. Both are essential if you have 10+ days.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="card p-5">
            <h3 class="heading-md text-lg text-[var(--color-text)] mb-3">Yunnan <span class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">vs</span> Tibet</h3>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 text-xs mb-3">
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Yunnan</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Diverse landscapes, 25 ethnic minorities, Tiger Leaping Gorge, ancient towns. No special permit needed.</p></div>
              <div><span class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-text)]">Tibet</span><p class="text-[var(--color-text-secondary)] mt-1">Potala Palace, Mount Everest Base Camp, high-altitude monasteries. Requires special permit and guided tour.</p></div>
            </div>
            <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-accent)] italic">Verdict: Yunnan for independent travelers. Tibet for the truly adventurous with more planning and budget.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
